use ../../lib/UiBehavior

export ui-behavior Selection:
    description:
        Selection keeps track of which items in a list the user has picked,
        and what picking one does to the others.

    modes:
        A list lets the user pick nothing, one item, or any number of items.
        This is set once for the list and does not change while it is in use.

    single:
        Picking an item replaces whatever was picked before. Whether picking
        the already-picked item un-picks it is up to the component. If the
        list must always have a value, it can't be un-picked.

    multiple:
        Picking an item adds it, or removes it if it was already picked, and
        leaves the others alone. Holding Shift (or the platform's equivalent)
        while picking selects every item between that one and the last item
        picked without Shift. The user can also select every item at once.
        Which keys do this is up to the chosen library and platform.

    disabledItems:
        The user can't pick or un-pick a disabled item, and Shift-selecting or
        selecting everything skips over it. A disabled item that was already
        picked stays picked. The component itself can still change it.

    focusVersusSelection:
        Where focus is and what is picked are separate. Moving through the
        list with the keyboard does not pick anything unless the component
        says it should. A radio group is the usual example of one that does.

    nonGoals:
        - Picks don't outlive the items. If an item is removed from the list,
            it is no longer picked
        - No limits on how many items can be picked. "Pick at most three" is a
            rule for the component or form to check
